From 6a185b9e2505236522110446c88f362d2b23b38e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ben Walker <ben@nvidia.com>
Date: Fri, 30 May 2025 10:42:32 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 05/17] sock/uring: Remove spdk_pipe support

This will hurt performance in some cases, so it cannot be merged without
the following patches in this series. However, it is a required change
to support zero copy rx.

Without the pipe, unread data stays in buffers borrowed from the group's
buffer ring rather than in a buffer owned by the socket. That changes
what happens when a socket leaves a group with data still buffered.
Previously the group buffer ring was only populated when the pipe was
disabled, which is not the default, so `recv_stream` was always empty at
detach and the existing assert in
`uring_sock_group_impl_remove_sock()` was unreachable. It is reachable
now: `test/nvmf/target/connect_cleanup.sh` writes 128 bytes of garbage
and closes, and the target rejects the PDU common header and destroys
the qpair from inside the socket callback with the remaining bytes still
unread.

Add `uring_sock_stage_recv_stream()`, which copies whatever is left into
a buffer owned by the socket and releases the group's buffers on the
thread that owns them, before the socket is detached. The copy goes back
on the stream as an ordinary tracker marked with
`URING_TRACKER_ID_STAGED`, so the receive paths need no special case and
only `uring_sock_release_tracker()` has to tell the two kinds apart.
`uring_sock_group_impl_attach_sock()` puts the socket back on
`pending_recv` when it arrives with staged data, so a socket migrating
between poll groups doesn't silently drop it.

Change-Id: Icb6b6a49490b749547b1ee7eeff9de2107c3d09c
Signed-off-by: Ben Walker <ben@nvidia.com>
